<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Merged over the Info.plist Tauri generates for the macOS bundle. It sets ONE key, and that key is
  the difference between an update every existing macOS user can take and one none of them can.

  ── THE STRANDING, AND WHY A CONSTANT FIXES IT ─────────────────────────────────────────────

  macOS shipped a different application until now — a SwiftUI client with the same bundle identifier
  (io.ohmail.desktop) and Sparkle as its updater. Sparkle decides whether a feed item is newer by
  comparing the appcast's `sparkle:version` against the INSTALLED app's CFBundleVersion, and that
  client stamped CFBundleVersion with a monotonic build number: `git rev-list --count HEAD` over the
  published repository. The releases that exist carry 251 (v0.5.0) through 291 (v0.6.1).

  Tauri, left alone, writes CFBundleVersion from the config's `version` — so this app would announce
  itself as `0.7.0`. Sparkle would compare 0.7.0 against 291, read the handover as a DOWNGRADE, and
  refuse it. Not loudly: every installed macOS copy would simply keep reporting itself up to date,
  for ever, with no error anywhere and nothing on screen to say why. That is the single worst outcome
  available in this release, because it is silent and it is permanent.

  So CFBundleVersion is pinned to an integer far above any build number that was ever published. It
  is not a version anybody reads: CFBundleShortVersionString stays `0.7.0` and is what the app, the
  appcast's display title and every download page show.

  ── WHY IT NEVER HAS TO CHANGE AGAIN ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  It is load-bearing for exactly one transition. Once a Mac has taken the handover it is running this
  app, and this app does not use Sparkle at all — it checks `latest.json` and compares its own
  compiled-in `0.7.0` against the feed's semver, never looking at CFBundleVersion. So the constant
  can stay where it is release after release; holding it still is what keeps the ONE comparison that
  matters unambiguous, and bumping it would only invite the mistake of bumping it wrongly.

  The floor is asserted where it is used, in `release-feeds.yml`: the appcast job refuses to publish
  an item whose sparkle:version is not an integer above the last Swift build number, so a plist that
  loses this key fails the release instead of stranding the people it was written for.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>100000</string>
</dict>
</plist>
